[s0l0!-header][h1]Conditionals[/h1][/s0l0!-header]

Conditionals are used to perform different computations or actions depending on whether a condition evaluates to true or false.

[h2]The if Statement[/h2]
The [b]if[/b] statement is called a [b]conditional control structure[/b] because it executes statements when an expression is true. For this reason, the [b]if[/b] is also known as a [b]decision structure[/b]. It takes the form: [code format="c" ]if (expression)
  statements[/code]
The expression evaluates to either [b]true [/b]or [b]false[/b], and statements can be a single statement or a code block enclosed by curly braces { }.
[b][/b][s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1631"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  int score = 89;
  
  [b]if [/b](score > 75)
    printf("You passed.\n");
    
  return 0;
}[/code]
In the code above we check whether the [b]score [/b]variable is greater than 75, and print a message if the condition is true.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

