[s0l0!-header][h1]Conditional Expressions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Another way to form an if-else statement is by using the ?: operator in a [b]conditional expression[/b]. The ?: operator can have only one statement associated with the[b] if[/b] and the [b]else[/b].
[b][/b][s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1634"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  int y;
  int x = 3;

  [b]y = (x >= 5) ?  5 : x;[/b]

/* This is equivalent to:
  if (x >= 5)
    y = 5;
  else
    y = x;
*/

  return 0;
}[/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

